{"transcript":1,"resume":null,"folder":".claude-work","todoTools":"1","arguments":["-p","--input-format","stream-json","--output-format","stream-json","--verbose","--include-partial-messages","--forward-subagent-text","--replay-user-messages","--permission-mode","default","--permission-prompt-tool","mcp__avala__permission_prompt","--mcp-config","{\u0022mcpServers\u0022:{\u0022avala\u0022:{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022sdk\u0022,\u0022name\u0022:\u0022avala\u0022,\u0022timeout\u0022:2147483647,\u0022disableAutoBackground\u0022:true}}}","--setting-sources","user,project,local","--settings","{\u0022disableAllHooks\u0022:true,\u0022claudeMdExcludes\u0022:[\u0022/home/user/.claude/**\u0022]}","--append-system-prompt","Avala shows the person your work inside its conversation. When you are asked to show something visual, such as a chart, a diagram or a drawing, call the mcp__avala__canvas tool with SVG or Mermaid: Avala draws it right there and plays its SVG animations written in SMIL, such as \u003Canimate\u003E or \u003CanimateTransform\u003E, so a chart may grow or a flow may move. Do not write an HTML file or open a browser to show it, unless it must respond to input, such as an interactive chart, a three.js scene or an app: then make it a page, as follows. For a page or an app, keep every file in the worktree, scratch files included, and vendor its libraries into it instead of loading them from a CDN. Serve it bound to 127.0.0.1 on port $AVALA_PORT, or start its dev server there; the ports $AVALA_PORTS are yours. Then tell the person its URL, such as http://localhost:$AVALA_PORT: Avala shows it beside the conversation. Never open a browser or take screenshots.","--model","haiku"],"recorded":"Claude Code 2.1.304 behind a local proxy that answered the first two /v1/messages with 529 overloaded: it reports system/api_retry twice and then answers."}
